hi there Steve Kaufman here I was asked  to do a video today in fact about the  differences between learning Japanese  and Chinese so I always appreciate  getting specific requests it's always  nice to respond to you know listeners  viewers who are interested in the  particular subject so I thought I would  try to look at these differences both in  terms of how I study the two languages  and I'm going to throw Korean in as well  three Asian languages and what I think  are general differences in terms of  difficulty and so forth remember that I  studied Chinese as a full-time language  student paid for by the Canadian  government because I was a diplomat and  they were training me to learn Chinese I  had three hours in the morning and then  I had lots of hours full time to devote  to reading and listening and and  building up my skill level in Chinese I  lived in Hong Kong which was not a place  for Mandarin Chinese was spoken so I did  not have the benefit of being immersed  in the language in Japan I studied it  entirely on my own but I had the  advantage of living in Japan Korean I am  studying here in Vancouver so again I'm  on my own but again don't have the  language surrounding me so if we look at  first of all as you probably know or may  know I believe that the best way to  learn a language is to devote a lot of  time to listening and reading and  vocabulary accumulation you can speak  whenever you want to start speaking but  until you have a lot of words it's very  difficult to have meaningful  conversations and I like doing things  that are meaningful in the language now  with Chinese an immediate problem is  that until you have learned enough  characters you cannot read anything very  interesting or meaningful in the  language so that makes Chinese quite  difficult I made a special effort to  learn the most frequent 1,000 character  I have flashcards for them I used to  write them out on these checkered you  know exercise books where I had sort of  a primitive spaced repetition system  that I've described before where I would  write the character out seven or eight  ten times and then put it over you know  put the meaning or the pronunciation  over three or four columns to the right  pick up another flashcard do the same  thing until I ran into that first  character and I would do that literally  every day so that once I had the  thousand most common characters that I  deliberately wrote out longhand to try  to learn thereafter as I encountered  characters in my reading it was enough  to write them out five six seven ten  times of course you keep on forgetting  but forgetting rewriting relearning  seeing them in context and eventually  they started to stick with characters  after a while you start to get used to  the different components the radicals as  they're called so that that helps you  remember these characters you get better  and better at learning them when you  first start out it seems like an  impossible task to remember you know the  stroke order and strokes for 10 12 13  different strokes for just one character  so the reading in the case of Chinese  initially a big obstacle but once you  have the characters it becomes very easy  to build vocabulary because combinations  different combinations of the characters  that you now know it correspond to  different words the way we count words  in English so that was with regard to  reading in Japanese the now bear in mind  when I studied Chinese I had to write an  exam so I knew that after as it turned  out after less than a year I would have  to be able to translate newspaper  editorials from Chinese into English  from English into Chinese right a  diplomatic note so I had to focus on my  writing with Japanese I was studying it  only in order to be able to use it and  therefore I essentially never wrote it  so as long as I could read it I was  happy the problem in Japanese is that a  lot of the sort of begin  our material the easier reading material  is very 